It’s not the normal warnings of NA/NaN function evaluations which I tend to ignore after inspection of the fit, but the fatal gradient evaluations/short gradient vectors that cause the optimisation to fail without reaching any of the stopping conditions. And then in seed values when I can make it past to a stopping condition, I find that the Hessian is often non-invertible.

I tried using the tmbprofile function to inspect the likelihood, and in some cases I’ve noticed a big spike nll values (more positive) near the true values of the parameters, which I found to be confusing as heck, especially since I can’t find anything that would make the function non-smooth in that location. Hence, the grid search.

